13.1.4.3 Connectors
...................

Connectors are transports for aiohttp client API.

There are standard connectors:

  1. *note TCPConnector: 73. for regular 'TCP sockets' (both 'HTTP' and
     'HTTPS' schemes supported).

  2. *note UnixConnector: 76. for connecting via UNIX socket (it’s used
     mostly for testing purposes).

All connector classes should be derived from *note BaseConnector: 94.

By default all 'connectors' support 'keep-alive connections' (behavior
is controlled by 'force_close' constructor’s parameter).

 -- Class: aiohttp.BaseConnector (*, keepalive_timeout=15,
          force_close=False, limit=100, limit_per_host=0,
          enable_cleanup_closed=False, loop=None)

     Base class for all connectors.


     Parameters:

        * ‘keepalive_timeout’ (float(1)) – timeout for connection
          reusing after releasing (optional).  Values ‘0’.  For
          disabling 'keep-alive' feature use ‘force_close=True’ flag.

        * ‘limit’ (int(2)) – total number simultaneous connections.  If
          'limit' is ‘0’ the connector has no limit (default: 100).

        * ‘limit_per_host’ (int(3)) – limit simultaneous connections to
          the same endpoint.  Endpoints are the same if they are have
          equal ‘(host, port, is_ssl)’ triple.  If 'limit' is ‘0’ the
          connector has no limit (default: 0).

        * ‘force_close’ (bool(4)) – close underlying sockets after
          connection releasing (optional).

        * ‘enable_cleanup_closed’ (bool(5)) – some SSL servers do not
          properly complete SSL shutdown process, in that case asyncio
          leaks SSL connections.  If this parameter is set to True,
          aiohttp additionally aborts underlining transport after 2
          seconds.  It is off by default.

          For Python version 3.12.7+, or 3.13.1 and later, this
          parameter is ignored because the asyncio SSL connection leak
          is fixed in these versions of Python.

        * ‘loop’ – event loop(6) used for handling connections.  If
          param is ‘None’, asyncio.get_event_loop()(7) is used for
          getting default event loop.

          Deprecated since version 2.0.

      -- Attribute: closed

          Read-only property, ‘True’ if connector is closed.

      -- Attribute: force_close

          Read-only property, ‘True’ if connector should ultimately
          close connections on releasing.

      -- Attribute: limit

          The total number for simultaneous connections.  If limit is 0
          the connector has no limit.  The default limit size is 100.

      -- Attribute: limit_per_host

          The limit for simultaneous connections to the same endpoint.

          Endpoints are the same if they are have equal ‘(host, port,
          is_ssl)’ triple.

          If 'limit_per_host' is ‘0’ the connector has no limit per
          host.

          Read-only property.

      -- Method: async close ()

          Close all opened connections.

      -- Method: async connect (request)

          Get a free connection from pool or create new one if
          connection is absent in the pool.

          The call may be paused if *note limit: b6. is exhausted until
          used connections returns to pool.


          Parameters: ‘request’ (*note aiohttp.ClientRequest: 95.) –
          request object which is connection initiator.


          Returns: *note Connection: ba. object.

      -- Method: async _create_connection (req)

          Abstract method for actual connection establishing, should be
          overridden in subclasses.

 -- Class: aiohttp.AddrInfoType

     Refer to aiohappyeyeballs.AddrInfoType(8) for more info.

     Warning: Be sure to use ‘aiohttp.AddrInfoType’ rather than
     ‘aiohappyeyeballs.AddrInfoType’ to avoid import breakage, as it is
     likely to be removed from aiohappyeyeballs(9) in the future.

 -- Class: aiohttp.SocketFactoryType

     Refer to aiohappyeyeballs.SocketFactoryType(10) for more info.

