13.1.4.5 ClientWebSocketResponse
................................

To connect to a websocket server ‘aiohttp.ws_connect()’ or *note
aiohttp.ClientSession.ws_connect(): 45. coroutines should be used, do
not create an instance of class *note ClientWebSocketResponse: 46.
manually.

 -- Class: aiohttp.ClientWebSocketResponse

     Class for handling client-side websockets.

      -- Attribute: closed

          Read-only property, ‘True’ if *note close(): e2. has been
          called or *note CLOSE: e3. message has been received from
          peer.

      -- Attribute: protocol

          Websocket 'subprotocol' chosen after ‘start()’ call.

          May be ‘None’ if server and client protocols are not
          overlapping.

      -- Method: get_extra_info (name, default=None)

          Reads optional extra information from the connection’s
          transport.  If no value associated with ‘name’ is found,
          ‘default’ is returned.

          See asyncio.BaseTransport.get_extra_info()(1)


          Parameters:

             * ‘name’ (str(2)) – The key to look up in the transport
               extra information.

             * ‘default’ – Default value to be used when no value for
               ‘name’ is found (default is ‘None’).

      -- Method: exception ()

          Returns exception if any occurs or returns None.

      -- Method: async ping (message=b'')

          Send *note PING: e8. to peer.


          Parameters: ‘message’ – optional payload of 'ping' message,
          str(3) (converted to 'UTF-8' encoded bytes) or bytes(4).

          Changed in version 3.0: The method is converted into
          coroutine(5)

      -- Method: async pong (message=b'')

          Send *note PONG: ea. to peer.


          Parameters: ‘message’ – optional payload of 'pong' message,
          str(6) (converted to 'UTF-8' encoded bytes) or bytes(7).

          Changed in version 3.0: The method is converted into
          coroutine(8)

      -- Method: async send_str (data, compress=None)

          Send 'data' to peer as *note TEXT: ec. message.


          Parameters:

             * ‘data’ (str(9)) – data to send.

             * ‘compress’ (int(10)) – sets specific level of compression
               for single message, ‘None’ for not overriding per-socket
               setting.


          Raises: TypeError(11) – if data is not str(12)

          Changed in version 3.0: The method is converted into
          coroutine(13), 'compress' parameter added.

      -- Method: async send_bytes (data, compress=None)

          Send 'data' to peer as *note BINARY: ee. message.


          Parameters:

             * ‘data’ – data to send.

             * ‘compress’ (int(14)) – sets specific level of compression
               for single message, ‘None’ for not overriding per-socket
               setting.


          Raises: TypeError(15) – if data is not bytes(16),
          bytearray(17) or memoryview(18).

          Changed in version 3.0: The method is converted into
          coroutine(19), 'compress' parameter added.

      -- Method: async send_json (data, compress=None, *,
               dumps=json.dumps)

          Send 'data' to peer as JSON string.


          Parameters:

             * ‘data’ – data to send.

             * ‘compress’ (int(20)) – sets specific level of compression
               for single message, ‘None’ for not overriding per-socket
               setting.

             * ‘dumps’ (collections.abc.Callable(21)) – any *note
               callable: 99. that accepts an object and returns a JSON
               string (json.dumps()(22) by default).


          Raises:

             * RuntimeError(23) – if connection is not started or
               closing

             * ValueError(24) – if data is not serializable object

             * TypeError(25) – if value returned by ‘dumps(data)’ is not
               str(26)

          Changed in version 3.0: The method is converted into
          coroutine(27), 'compress' parameter added.

      -- Method: async send_frame (message, opcode, compress=None)

          Send a *note WSMsgType: f1. message 'message' to peer.

          This method is low-level and should be used with caution as it
          only accepts bytes which must conform to the correct message
          type for 'message'.

          It is recommended to use the *note send_str(): eb, *note
          send_bytes(): ed. or *note send_json(): ef. methods instead of
          this method.

          The primary use case for this method is to send bytes that are
          have already been encoded without having to decode and
          re-encode them.


          Parameters:

             * ‘message’ (bytes(28)) – message to send.

             * ‘opcode’ (*note WSMsgType: f1.) – opcode of the message.

             * ‘compress’ (int(29)) – sets specific level of compression
               for single message, ‘None’ for not overriding per-socket
               setting.

          Added in version 3.11.

      -- Method: async close (*, code=WSCloseCode.OK, message=b'')

          A coroutine(30) that initiates closing handshake by sending
          *note CLOSE: e3. message.  It waits for close response from
          server.  To add a timeout to ‘close()’ call just wrap the call
          with ‘asyncio.wait()’ or ‘asyncio.wait_for()’.


          Parameters:

             * ‘code’ (int(31)) – closing code.  See also *note
               WSCloseCode: f2.

             * ‘message’ – optional payload of 'close' message, str(32)
               (converted to 'UTF-8' encoded bytes) or bytes(33).

      -- Method: async receive ()

          A coroutine(34) that waits upcoming 'data' message from peer
          and returns it.

          The coroutine implicitly handles *note PING: e8, *note PONG:
          ea. and *note CLOSE: e3. without returning the message.

          It process 'ping-pong game' and performs 'closing handshake'
          internally.


          Returns: *note WSMessage: f4.

      -- Method: async receive_str ()

          A coroutine(35) that calls *note receive(): f3. but also
          asserts the message type is *note TEXT: ec.


          Return str: peer’s message content.


          Raises: *note aiohttp.WSMessageTypeError: f6. – if message is
          not *note TEXT: ec.

      -- Method: async receive_bytes ()

          A coroutine(36) that calls *note receive(): f3. but also
          asserts the message type is *note BINARY: ee.


          Return bytes: peer’s message content.


          Raises: *note aiohttp.WSMessageTypeError: f6. – if message is
          not *note BINARY: ee.

      -- Method: async receive_json (*, loads=json.loads)

          A coroutine(37) that calls *note receive_str(): f5. and loads
          the JSON string to a Python dict.


          Parameters: ‘loads’ (collections.abc.Callable(38)) – any *note
          callable: 99. that accepts str(39) and returns dict(40) with
          parsed JSON (json.loads()(41) by default).


          Return dict: loaded JSON content


          Raises:

             * TypeError(42) – if message is *note BINARY: ee.

             * ValueError(43) – if message is not valid JSON.

