13.2.2.8 Template Rendering
...........................

*note aiohttp.web: 3. does not support template rendering
out-of-the-box.

However, there is a third-party library, aiohttp_jinja2(1), which is
supported by the 'aiohttp' authors.

Using it is rather simple.  First, setup a 'jinja2 environment' with a
call to aiohttp_jinja2.setup()(2):

     app = web.Application()
     aiohttp_jinja2.setup(app,
         loader=jinja2.FileSystemLoader('/path/to/templates/folder'))

After that you may use the template engine in your *note handlers: 19a.
The most convenient way is to simply wrap your handlers with the
aiohttp_jinja2.template()(3) decorator:

     @aiohttp_jinja2.template('tmpl.jinja2')
     async def handler(request):
         return {'name': 'Andrew', 'surname': 'Svetlov'}

If you prefer the Mako(4) template engine, please take a look at the
aiohttp_mako(5) library.

     Warning: aiohttp_jinja2.template()(6) should be applied 'before'
     *note RouteTableDef.get(): 1c4. decorator and family, e.g.  it must
     be the 'first' (most 'down' decorator in the chain):

          @routes.get('/path')
          @aiohttp_jinja2.template('tmpl.jinja2')
          async def handler(request):
              return {'name': 'Andrew', 'surname': 'Svetlov'}

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

