13.2.4.1 Request and Base Request
.................................

The Request object contains all the information about an incoming HTTP
request.

*note BaseRequest: 235. is used for *note Low-Level Servers: 231. (which
have no applications, routers, signals and middlewares).  *note Request:
19b. has an *note Request.app: 23e. and *note Request.match_info: 1b3.
attributes.

A *note BaseRequest: 235. / *note Request: 19b. are dict(1) like
objects, allowing them to be used for *note sharing data: 1f0. among
*note Middlewares: d. and *note Signals: e. handlers.

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.BaseRequest

      -- Attribute: version

          'HTTP version' of request, Read-only property.

          Returns ‘aiohttp.protocol.HttpVersion’ instance.

      -- Attribute: method

          'HTTP method', read-only property.

          The value is upper-cased str(2) like ‘"GET"’, ‘"POST"’,
          ‘"PUT"’ etc.

      -- Attribute: url

          A URL(3) instance with absolute URL to resource ('scheme',
          'host' and 'port' are included).

               Note: In case of malformed request (e.g.  without
               ‘"HOST"’ HTTP header) the absolute url may be
               unavailable.

      -- Attribute: rel_url

          A URL(4) instance with relative URL to resource (contains
          'path', 'query' and 'fragment' parts only, 'scheme', 'host'
          and 'port' are excluded).

          The property is equal to ‘.url.relative()’ but is always
          present.

          See also
          ........

          A note from *note url: 240.

      -- Attribute: scheme

          A string representing the scheme of the request.

          The scheme is ‘'https'’ if transport for request handling is
          'SSL', ‘'http'’ otherwise.

          The value could be overridden by *note clone(): 22a.

          Read-only str(5) property.

          Changed in version 2.3: 'Forwarded' and 'X-Forwarded-Proto'
          are not used anymore.

          Call ‘.clone(scheme=new_scheme)’ for setting up the value
          explicitly.

          See also
          ........

          *note Deploying behind a Proxy: 222.

      -- Attribute: secure

          Shorthand for ‘request.url.scheme == 'https'’

          Read-only bool(6) property.

          See also
          ........

          *note scheme: 226.

      -- Attribute: forwarded

          A tuple containing all parsed Forwarded header(s).

          Makes an effort to parse Forwarded headers as specified by RFC
          7239(7):

             - It adds one (immutable) dictionary per Forwarded
               ‘field-value’, i.e.  per proxy.  The element corresponds
               to the data in the Forwarded ‘field-value’ added by the
               first proxy encountered by the client.  Each subsequent
               item corresponds to those added by later proxies.

             - It checks that every value has valid syntax in general as
               specified in RFC 7239 Section 4(8): either a ‘token’ or a
               ‘quoted-string’.

             - It un-escapes ‘quoted-pairs’.

             - It does NOT validate ‘by’ and ‘for’ contents as specified
               in RFC 7239 Section 6(9).

             - It does NOT validate ‘host’ contents (Host ABNF).

             - It does NOT validate ‘proto’ contents for valid URI
               scheme names.

          Returns a tuple containing one or more ‘MappingProxy’ objects

          See also
          ........

          *note scheme: 226.

          See also
          ........

          *note host: 227.

      -- Attribute: host

          Host name of the request, resolved in this order:

             - Overridden value by *note clone(): 22a. call.

             - 'Host' HTTP header

             - socket.getfqdn()(10)

          Read-only str(11) property.

          Changed in version 2.3: 'Forwarded' and 'X-Forwarded-Host' are
          not used anymore.

          Call ‘.clone(host=new_host)’ for setting up the value
          explicitly.

          See also
          ........

          *note Deploying behind a Proxy: 222.

      -- Attribute: remote

          Originating IP address of a client initiated HTTP request.

          The IP is resolved through the following headers, in this
          order:

             - Overridden value by *note clone(): 22a. call.

             - Peer name of opened socket.

          Read-only str(12) property.

          Call ‘.clone(remote=new_remote)’ for setting up the value
          explicitly.

          Added in version 2.3.

          See also
          ........

          *note Deploying behind a Proxy: 222.

      -- Attribute: client_max_size

          The maximum size of the request body.

          The value could be overridden by *note clone(): 22a.

          Read-only int(13) property.

      -- Attribute: path_qs

          The URL including PATH_INFO and the query string.  e.g.,
          ‘/app/blog?id=10’

          Read-only str(14) property.

      -- Attribute: path

          The URL including 'PATH INFO' without the host or scheme.
          e.g., ‘/app/blog’.  The path is URL-decoded.  For raw path
          info see *note raw_path: 246.

          Read-only str(15) property.

      -- Attribute: raw_path

          The URL including raw 'PATH INFO' without the host or scheme.
          Warning, the path may be URL-encoded and may contain invalid
          URL characters, e.g.
          ‘/my%2Fpath%7Cwith%21some%25strange%24characters’.

          For URL-decoded version please take a look on *note path: 245.

          Read-only str(16) property.

      -- Attribute: query

          A multidict with all the variables in the query string.

          Read-only MultiDictProxy(17) lazy property.

      -- Attribute: query_string

          The query string in the URL, e.g., ‘id=10’

          Read-only str(18) property.

      -- Attribute: headers

          A case-insensitive multidict proxy with all headers.

          Read-only CIMultiDictProxy(19) property.

      -- Attribute: raw_headers

          HTTP headers of response as unconverted bytes, a sequence of
          ‘(key, value)’ pairs.

      -- Attribute: keep_alive

          ‘True’ if keep-alive connection enabled by HTTP client and
          protocol version supports it, otherwise ‘False’.

          Read-only bool(20) property.

      -- Attribute: transport

          A transport(21) used to process request.  Read-only property.

          The property can be used, for example, for getting IP address
          of client’s peer:

               peername = request.transport.get_extra_info('peername')
               if peername is not None:
                   host, port = peername

      -- Attribute: loop

          An event loop instance used by HTTP request handling.

          Read-only asyncio.AbstractEventLoop(22) property.

          Deprecated since version 3.5.

      -- Attribute: cookies

          A read-only dictionary-like object containing the request’s
          cookies.

          Read-only MappingProxyType(23) property.

      -- Attribute: content

          A *note StreamReader: 3f. instance, input stream for reading
          request’s 'BODY'.

          Read-only property.

      -- Attribute: body_exists

          Return ‘True’ if request has 'HTTP BODY', ‘False’ otherwise.

          Read-only bool(24) property.

          Added in version 2.3.

      -- Attribute: can_read_body

          Return ‘True’ if request’s 'HTTP BODY' can be read, ‘False’
          otherwise.

          Read-only bool(25) property.

          Added in version 2.3.

      -- Attribute: has_body

          Return ‘True’ if request’s 'HTTP BODY' can be read, ‘False’
          otherwise.

          Read-only bool(26) property.

          Deprecated since version 2.3: Use *note can_read_body(): 24f.
          instead.

      -- Attribute: content_type

          Read-only property with 'content' part of 'Content-Type'
          header.

          Returns str(27) like ‘'text/html'’

               Note: Returns value is ‘'application/octet-stream'’ if no
               Content-Type header present in HTTP headers according to
               RFC 2616(28)

      -- Attribute: charset

          Read-only property that specifies the 'encoding' for the
          request’s BODY.

          The value is parsed from the 'Content-Type' HTTP header.

          Returns str(29) like ‘'utf-8'’ or ‘None’ if 'Content-Type' has
          no charset information.

      -- Attribute: content_length

          Read-only property that returns length of the request’s BODY.

          The value is parsed from the 'Content-Length' HTTP header.

          Returns int(30) or ‘None’ if 'Content-Length' is absent.

      -- Attribute: http_range

          Read-only property that returns information about 'Range' HTTP
          header.

          Returns a slice(31) where ‘.start’ is 'left inclusive bound',
          ‘.stop’ is 'right exclusive bound' and ‘.step’ is ‘1’.

          The property might be used in two manners:

            1. Attribute-access style (example assumes that both left
               and right borders are set, the real logic for case of
               open bounds is more complex):

                    rng = request.http_range
                    with open(filename, 'rb') as f:
                        f.seek(rng.start)
                        return f.read(rng.stop-rng.start)

            2. Slice-style:

                    return buffer[request.http_range]

      -- Attribute: if_modified_since

          Read-only property that returns the date specified in the
          'If-Modified-Since' header.

          Returns datetime.datetime(32) or ‘None’ if 'If-Modified-Since'
          header is absent or is not a valid HTTP date.

      -- Attribute: if_unmodified_since

          Read-only property that returns the date specified in the
          'If-Unmodified-Since' header.

          Returns datetime.datetime(33) or ‘None’ if
          'If-Unmodified-Since' header is absent or is not a valid HTTP
          date.

          Added in version 3.1.

      -- Attribute: if_match

          Read-only property that returns *note ETag: 111. objects
          specified in the 'If-Match' header.

          Returns tuple(34) of *note ETag: 111. or ‘None’ if 'If-Match'
          header is absent.

          Added in version 3.8.

      -- Attribute: if_none_match

          Read-only property that returns *note ETag: 111. objects
          specified 'If-None-Match' header.

          Returns tuple(35) of *note ETag: 111. or ‘None’ if
          'If-None-Match' header is absent.

          Added in version 3.8.

      -- Attribute: if_range

          Read-only property that returns the date specified in the
          'If-Range' header.

          Returns datetime.datetime(36) or ‘None’ if 'If-Range' header
          is absent or is not a valid HTTP date.

          Added in version 3.1.

      -- Method: clone (*, method=..., rel_url=..., headers=...)

          Clone itself with replacement some attributes.

          Creates and returns a new instance of Request object.  If no
          parameters are given, an exact copy is returned.  If a
          parameter is not passed, it will reuse the one from the
          current request object.


          Parameters:

             * ‘method’ (str(37)) – http method

             * ‘rel_url’ – url to use, str(38) or URL(39)

             * ‘headers’ – CIMultiDict(40) or compatible headers
               container.


          Returns: a cloned *note Request: 19b. instance.

      -- Method: get_extra_info (name, default=None)

          Reads extra information from the protocol’s transport.  If no
          value associated with ‘name’ is found, ‘default’ is returned.

          See asyncio.BaseTransport.get_extra_info()(41)


          Parameters:

             * ‘name’ (str(42)) – The key to look up in the transport
               extra information.

             * ‘default’ – Default value to be used when no value for
               ‘name’ is found (default is ‘None’).

          Added in version 3.7.

      -- Method: async read ()

          Read request body, returns bytes(43) object with body content.

               Note: The method 'does' store read data internally,
               subsequent *note read(): 203. call will return the same
               value.

      -- Method: async text ()

          Read request body, decode it using *note charset: 252.
          encoding or ‘UTF-8’ if no encoding was specified in
          'MIME-type'.

          Returns str(44) with body content.

               Note: The method 'does' store read data internally,
               subsequent *note text(): 204. call will return the same
               value.

      -- Method: async json (*, loads=json.loads)

          Read request body decoded as 'json'.

          The method is just a boilerplate coroutine(45) implemented as:

               async def json(self, *, loads=json.loads):
                   body = await self.text()
                   return loads(body)


          Parameters: ‘loads’ (collections.abc.Callable(46)) – any *note
          callable: 99. that accepts str(47) and returns dict(48) with
          parsed JSON (json.loads()(49) by default).

               Note: The method 'does' store read data internally,
               subsequent *note json(): 205. call will return the same
               value.

      -- Method: async multipart ()

          Returns *note aiohttp.MultipartReader: 25b. which processes
          incoming 'multipart' request.

          The method is just a boilerplate coroutine(50) implemented as:

               async def multipart(self, *, reader=aiohttp.multipart.MultipartReader):
                   return reader(self.headers, self._payload)

          This method is a coroutine for consistency with the else
          reader methods.

               Warning: The method 'does not' store read data
               internally.  That means once you exhausts multipart
               reader, you cannot get the request payload one more time.

          See also
          ........

          *note Working with Multipart: 42.

          Changed in version 3.4: Dropped 'reader' parameter.

      -- Method: async post ()

          A coroutine(51) that reads POST parameters from request body.

          Returns MultiDictProxy(52) instance filled with parsed data.

          If *note method: 1ab. is not 'POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'TRACE'
          or 'DELETE' or *note content_type: 251. is not empty or
          'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' or 'multipart/form-data'
          returns empty multidict.

               Note: The method 'does' store read data internally,
               subsequent *note post(): 1cd. call will return the same
               value.

      -- Method: async release ()

          Release request.

          Eat unread part of HTTP BODY if present.

               Note: User code may never call *note release(): 25c, all
               required work will be processed by *note aiohttp.web: 3.
               internal machinery.

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.Request

     A request used for receiving request’s information by 'web
     handler'.

     Every *note handler: 19a. accepts a request instance as the first
     positional parameter.

     The class in derived from *note BaseRequest: 235, shares all
     parent’s attributes and methods but has a couple of additional
     properties:

      -- Attribute: match_info

          Read-only property with *note AbstractMatchInfo: 25d. instance
          for result of route resolving.

               Note: Exact type of property depends on used router.  If
               ‘app.router’ is *note UrlDispatcher: 1b0. the property
               contains *note UrlMappingMatchInfo: 25e. instance.

      -- Attribute: app

          An *note Application: 19d. instance used to call *note request
          handler: 19a, Read-only property.

      -- Attribute: config_dict

          A *note aiohttp.ChainMapProxy: 25f. instance for mapping all
          properties from the current application returned by *note app:
          23e. property and all its parents.

          See also
          ........

          *note Application’s config: 1f2.

          Added in version 3.2.

          Note: You should never create the *note Request: 19b. instance
          manually – *note aiohttp.web: 3. does it for you.  But *note
          clone(): 22a. may be used for cloning 'modified' request copy
          with changed 'path', 'method' etc.

