13.2.4.2 Response classes
.........................

For now, *note aiohttp.web: 3. has three classes for the 'HTTP
response': *note StreamResponse: 1a5, *note Response: 19c. and *note
FileResponse: 262.

Usually you need to use the second one.  *note StreamResponse: 1a5. is
intended for streaming data, while *note Response: 19c. contains 'HTTP
BODY' as an attribute and sends own content as single piece with the
correct 'Content-Length HTTP header'.

For sake of design decisions *note Response: 19c. is derived from *note
StreamResponse: 1a5. parent class.

The response supports 'keep-alive' handling out-of-the-box if 'request'
supports it.

You can disable 'keep-alive' by *note force_close(): 263. though.

The common case for sending an answer from *note web-handler: 19a. is
returning a *note Response: 19c. instance:

     async def handler(request):
         return Response(text="All right!")

Response classes are dict(1) like objects, allowing them to be used for
*note sharing data: 1f0. among *note Middlewares: d. and *note Signals:
e. handlers:

     resp['key'] = value

Added in version 3.0: Dict-like interface support.

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.StreamResponse (*, status=200, reason=None)

     The base class for the 'HTTP response' handling.

     Contains methods for setting 'HTTP response headers', 'cookies',
     'response status code', writing 'HTTP response BODY' and so on.

     The most important thing you should know about 'response' — it is
     'Finite State Machine'.

     That means you can do any manipulations with 'headers', 'cookies'
     and 'status code' only before *note prepare(): 20a. coroutine is
     called.

     Once you call *note prepare(): 20a. any change of the 'HTTP header'
     part will raise RuntimeError(2) exception.

     Any *note write(): 236. call after *note write_eof(): 264. is also
     forbidden.


     Parameters:

        * ‘status’ (int(3)) – HTTP status code, ‘200’ by default.

        * ‘reason’ (str(4)) – HTTP reason.  If param is ‘None’ reason
          will be calculated basing on 'status' parameter.  Otherwise
          pass str(5) with arbitrary 'status' explanation..

      -- Attribute: prepared

          Read-only bool(6) property, ‘True’ if *note prepare(): 20a.
          has been called, ‘False’ otherwise.

      -- Attribute: task

          A task that serves HTTP request handling.

          May be useful for graceful shutdown of long-running requests
          (streaming, long polling or web-socket).

      -- Attribute: status

          Read-only property for 'HTTP response status code', int(7).

          ‘200’ (OK) by default.

      -- Attribute: reason

          Read-only property for 'HTTP response reason', str(8).

      -- Method: set_status (status, reason=None)

          Set *note status: 267. and *note reason: 268.

          'reason' value is auto calculated if not specified (‘None’).

