 -- Class: aiohttp.web.Application (*, logger=<default>, router=None,
          middlewares=(), handler_args=None, client_max_size=1024**2,
          loop=None, debug=...)

     Application is a synonym for web-server.

     To get a fully working example, you have to make an 'application',
     register supported urls in the 'router' and pass it to *note
     aiohttp.web.run_app(): 19e. or *note aiohttp.web.AppRunner: 215.

     'Application' contains a 'router' instance and a list of callbacks
     that will be called during application finishing.

     This class is a dict(1)-like object, so you can use it for *note
     sharing data: 1f0. globally by storing arbitrary properties for
     later access from a *note handler: 19a. via the *note Request.app:
     23e. property:

          app = Application()
          database = AppKey("database", AsyncEngine)
          app[database] = await create_async_engine(db_url)

          async def handler(request):
              async with request.app[database].begin() as conn:
                  await conn.execute("DELETE * FROM table")

     Although it' is a dict(2)-like object, it can’t be duplicated like
     one using ‘copy()’.

     The class inherits dict(3).


     Parameters:

        * ‘logger’ – logging.Logger(4) instance for storing application
          logs.

          By default the value is ‘logging.getLogger("aiohttp.web")’

        * ‘router’ –

          *note aiohttp.abc.AbstractRouter: 293. instance, the system

               creates *note UrlDispatcher: 1b0. by default if 'router'
               is ‘None’.

          Deprecated since version 3.3: The custom routers support is
          deprecated, the parameter will be removed in 4.0.

        * ‘middlewares’ – list(5) of middleware factories, see *note
          Middlewares: d. for details.

        * ‘handler_args’ – dict-like object that overrides keyword
          arguments of *note Application.make_handler(): 294.

        * ‘client_max_size’ – client’s maximum size in a request, in
          bytes.  If a POST request exceeds this value, it raises an
          ‘HTTPRequestEntityTooLarge’ exception.

        * ‘loop’ – event loop

          Deprecated since version 2.0: The parameter is deprecated.
          Loop is get set during freeze stage.

        * ‘debug’ – Switches debug mode.

          Deprecated since version 3.5: Use asyncio Debug Mode(6)
          instead.

      -- Attribute: router

          Read-only property that returns 'router instance'.

      -- Attribute: logger

          logging.Logger(7) instance for storing application logs.

      -- Attribute: loop

          event loop(8) used for processing HTTP requests.

          Deprecated since version 3.5.

      -- Attribute: debug

          Boolean value indicating whether the debug mode is turned on
          or off.

          Deprecated since version 3.5: Use asyncio Debug Mode(9)
          instead.

      -- Attribute: on_response_prepare

          A Signal(10) that is fired near the end of *note
          StreamResponse.prepare(): 20a. with parameters 'request' and
          'response'.  It can be used, for example, to add custom
          headers to each response, or to modify the default headers
          computed by the application, directly before sending the
          headers to the client.

          Signal handlers should have the following signature:

               async def on_prepare(request, response):
                   pass

               Note: The headers are written immediately after these
               callbacks are run.  Therefore, if you modify the content
               of the response, you may need to adjust the
               ‘Content-Length’ header or similar to match.  Aiohttp
               will not make any updates to the headers from this point.

      -- Attribute: on_startup

          A Signal(11) that is fired on application start-up.

          Subscribers may use the signal to run background tasks in the
          event loop along with the application’s request handler just
          after the application start-up.

          Signal handlers should have the following signature:

               async def on_startup(app):
                   pass

          See also
          ........

          *note Signals: e.

      -- Attribute: on_shutdown

          A Signal(12) that is fired on application shutdown.

          Subscribers may use the signal for gracefully closing long
          running connections, e.g.  websockets and data streaming.

          Signal handlers should have the following signature:

               async def on_shutdown(app):
                   pass

          It’s up to end user to figure out which *note web-handler:
          19f.s are still alive and how to finish them properly.

          We suggest keeping a list of long running handlers in *note
          Application: 19d. dictionary.

          See also
          ........

          *note Graceful shutdown: 1a0. and *note on_cleanup: 200.

      -- Attribute: on_cleanup

          A Signal(13) that is fired on application cleanup.

          Subscribers may use the signal for gracefully closing
          connections to database server etc.

          Signal handlers should have the following signature:

               async def on_cleanup(app):
                   pass

          See also
          ........

          *note Signals: e. and *note on_shutdown: 1ff.

      -- Attribute: cleanup_ctx

          A list of 'context generators' for 'startup'/'cleanup'
          handling.

          Signal handlers should have the following signature:

               async def context(app):
                   # do startup stuff
                   yield
                   # do cleanup

          Added in version 3.1.

          See also
          ........

          *note Cleanup Context: 20c.

      -- Method: add_subapp (prefix, subapp)

          Register nested sub-application under given path 'prefix'.

          In resolving process if request’s path starts with 'prefix'
          then further resolving is passed to 'subapp'.


          Parameters:

             * ‘prefix’ (str(14)) – path’s prefix for the resource.

             * ‘subapp’ (*note Application: 19d.) – nested application
               attached under 'prefix'.


          Returns: a *note PrefixedSubAppResource: 298. instance.

      -- Method: add_domain (domain, subapp)

          Register nested sub-application that serves the domain name or
          domain name mask.

          In resolving process if request.headers[‘host’] matches the
          pattern 'domain' then further resolving is passed to 'subapp'.

               Warning: Registering many domains using this method may
               cause performance issues with handler routing.  If you
               have a substantial number of applications for different
               domains, you may want to consider using a reverse proxy
               (such as Nginx) to handle routing to different apps,
               rather that registering them as sub-applications.


          Parameters:

             * ‘domain’ (str(15)) – domain or mask of domain for the
               resource.

             * ‘subapp’ (*note Application: 19d.) – nested application.


          Returns: a ‘MatchedSubAppResource’ instance.

      -- Method: add_routes (routes_table)

          Register route definitions from 'routes_table'.

          The table is a list(16) of *note RouteDef: 1c1. items or *note
          RouteTableDef: 1c3.


          Returns: list(17) of registered *note AbstractRoute: 29a.
          instances.

          The method is a shortcut for
          ‘app.router.add_routes(routes_table)’, see also *note
          UrlDispatcher.add_routes(): 1c0.

          Added in version 3.1.

          Changed in version 3.7: Return value updated from ‘None’ to
          list(18) of *note AbstractRoute: 29a. instances.

      -- Method: make_handler (loop=None, **kwargs)

          Creates HTTP protocol factory for handling requests.


          Parameters:

             * ‘loop’ – event loop(19) used for processing HTTP
               requests.

               If param is ‘None’ asyncio.get_event_loop()(20) used for
               getting default event loop.

               Deprecated since version 2.0.

             * ‘tcp_keepalive’ (bool(21)) – Enable TCP Keep-Alive.
               Default: ‘True’.

             * ‘keepalive_timeout’ (int(22)) – Number of seconds before
               closing Keep-Alive connection.  Default: ‘75’ seconds
               (NGINX’s default value).

             * ‘logger’ – Custom logger object.  Default:
               ‘aiohttp.log.server_logger’.

             * ‘access_log’ – Custom logging object.  Default:
               ‘aiohttp.log.access_logger’.

             * ‘access_log_class’ – Class for ‘access_logger’.  Default:
               ‘aiohttp.helpers.AccessLogger’.  Must to be a subclass of
               *note aiohttp.abc.AbstractAccessLogger: 29b.

             * ‘access_log_format’ (str(23)) – Access log format string.
               Default: ‘helpers.AccessLogger.LOG_FORMAT’.

             * ‘max_line_size’ (int(24)) – Optional maximum header line
               size.  Default: ‘8190’.

             * ‘max_headers’ (int(25)) – Optional maximum header size.
               Default: ‘32768’.

             * ‘max_field_size’ (int(26)) – Optional maximum header
               field size.  Default: ‘8190’.

             * ‘lingering_time’ (float(27)) – Maximum time during which
               the server reads and ignores additional data coming from
               the client when lingering close is on.  Use ‘0’ to
               disable lingering on server channel closing.

          You should pass result of the method as 'protocol_factory' to
          ‘create_server()’, e.g.:

               loop = asyncio.get_event_loop()

               app = Application()

               # setup route table
               # app.router.add_route(...)

               await loop.create_server(app.make_handler(),
                                        '0.0.0.0', 8080)

          Deprecated since version 3.2: The method is deprecated and
          will be removed in future aiohttp versions.  Please use *note
          Application runners: 1a1. instead.

      -- Method: async startup ()

          A coroutine(28) that will be called along with the
          application’s request handler.

          The purpose of the method is calling *note on_startup: 1fe.
          signal handlers.

      -- Method: async shutdown ()

          A coroutine(29) that should be called on server stopping but
          before *note cleanup(): 29d.

          The purpose of the method is calling *note on_shutdown: 1ff.
          signal handlers.

      -- Method: async cleanup ()

          A coroutine(30) that should be called on server stopping but
          after *note shutdown(): 29c.

          The purpose of the method is calling *note on_cleanup: 200.
          signal handlers.

          Note: Application object has *note router: 1af. attribute but
          has no ‘add_route()’ method.  The reason is: we want to
          support different router implementations (even maybe not
          url-matching based but traversal ones).

          For sake of that fact we have very trivial ABC for *note
          AbstractRouter: 293.: it should have only *note
          aiohttp.abc.AbstractRouter.resolve(): 29e. coroutine.

          No methods for adding routes or route reversing (getting URL
          by route name).  All those are router implementation details
          (but, sure, you need to deal with that methods after choosing
          the router for your application).

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.AppKey (name, t)

     This class should be used for the keys in *note Application: 19d.
     They provide a type-safe alternative to ‘str’ keys when checking
     your code with a type checker (e.g.  mypy).  They also avoid name
     clashes with keys from different libraries etc.


     Parameters:

        * ‘name’ – A name to help with debugging.  This should be the
          same as the variable name (much like how typing.TypeVar(31) is
          used).

        * ‘t’ – The type that should be used for the value in the dict
          (e.g.  ‘str’, ‘Iterator[int]’ etc.)

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.Server

     A protocol factory compatible with ‘create_server()’.

     The class is responsible for creating HTTP protocol objects that
     can handle HTTP connections.

      -- Attribute: connections

          List of all currently opened connections.

      -- Attribute: requests_count

          Amount of processed requests.

      -- Method: async shutdown (timeout)

          A coroutine(32) that should be called to close all opened
          connections.

 -- Class: aiohttp.web.UrlDispatcher

     For dispatching URLs to *note handlers: 19a. *note aiohttp.web: 3.
     uses 'routers', which is any object that implements *note
     AbstractRouter: 293. interface.

     This class is a straightforward url-matching router, implementing
     collections.abc.Mapping(33) for access to 'named routes'.

     *note Application: 19d. uses this class as *note router(): 1af. by
     default.

     Before running an *note Application: 19d. you should fill 'route
     table' first by calling *note add_route(): 2a2. and *note
     add_static(): 2a3.

     *note Handler: 19a. lookup is performed by iterating on added
     'routes' in FIFO order.  The first matching 'route' will be used to
     call the corresponding 'handler'.

     If during route creation you specify 'name' parameter the result is
     a 'named route'.

     A 'named route' can be retrieved by a ‘app.router[name]’ call,
     checking for existence can be done with ‘name in app.router’ etc.

     See also
     ........

     *note Route classes: 2a4.

      -- Method: add_resource (path, *, name=None)

          Append a *note resource: 2a6. to the end of route table.

          'path' may be either 'constant' string like ‘'/a/b/c'’ or
          'variable rule' like ‘'/a/{var}'’ (see *note handling variable
          paths: 1b1.)


          Parameters:

             * ‘path’ (str(34)) – resource path spec.

             * ‘name’ (str(35)) – optional resource name.


          Returns: created resource instance (*note PlainResource: 2a7.
          or *note DynamicResource: 2a8.).

      -- Method: add_route (method, path, handler, *, name=None,
               expect_handler=None)

          Append *note handler: 19a. to the end of route table.

          'path' may be either 'constant' string like ‘'/a/b/c'’ or

               'variable rule' like ‘'/a/{var}'’ (see *note handling
               variable paths: 1b1.)

          Pay attention please: 'handler' is converted to coroutine
          internally when it is a regular function.


          Parameters:

             * ‘method’ (str(36)) – HTTP method for route.  Should be
               one of ‘'GET'’, ‘'POST'’, ‘'PUT'’, ‘'DELETE'’, ‘'PATCH'’,
               ‘'HEAD'’, ‘'OPTIONS'’ or ‘'*'’ for any method.

               The parameter is case-insensitive, e.g.  you can push
               ‘'get'’ as well as ‘'GET'’.

             * ‘path’ (str(37)) – route path.  Should be started with
               slash (‘'/'’).

             * ‘handler’ (collections.abc.Callable(38)) – route handler.

             * ‘name’ (str(39)) – optional route name.

             * ‘expect_handler’ (collections.abc.Coroutine(40)) –
               optional 'expect' header handler.


          Returns: new *note AbstractRoute: 29a. instance.

      -- Method: add_routes (routes_table)

          Register route definitions from 'routes_table'.

          The table is a list(41) of *note RouteDef: 1c1. items or *note
          RouteTableDef: 1c3.


          Returns: list(42) of registered *note AbstractRoute: 29a.
          instances.

          Added in version 2.3.

          Changed in version 3.7: Return value updated from ‘None’ to
          list(43) of *note AbstractRoute: 29a. instances.

      -- Method: add_get (path, handler, *, name=None, allow_head=True,
               **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a GET handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'GET'’.

          If 'allow_head' is ‘True’ (default) the route for method HEAD
          is added with the same handler as for GET.

          If 'name' is provided the name for HEAD route is suffixed with
          ‘'-head'’.  For example ‘router.add_get(path, handler,
          name='route')’ call adds two routes: first for GET with name
          ‘'route'’ and second for HEAD with name ‘'route-head'’.

      -- Method: add_post (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a POST handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with

          ‘method’ equals to ‘'POST'’.

      -- Method: add_head (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a HEAD handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'HEAD'’.

      -- Method: add_put (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a PUT handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'PUT'’.

      -- Method: add_patch (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a PATCH handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'PATCH'’.

      -- Method: add_delete (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a DELETE handler.  Calls the *note
          add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'DELETE'’.

      -- Method: add_view (path, handler, **kwargs)

          Shortcut for adding a class-based view handler.  Calls the
          *note add_route(): 2a2. with ‘method’ equals to ‘'*'’.

          Added in version 3.0.

      -- Method: add_static (prefix, path, *, name=None,
               expect_handler=None, chunk_size=256 * 1024,
               response_factory=StreamResponse, show_index=False,
               follow_symlinks=False, append_version=False)

          Adds a router and a handler for returning static files.

          Useful for serving static content like images, javascript and
          css files.

          On platforms that support it, the handler will transfer files
          more efficiently using the ‘sendfile’ system call.

          In some situations it might be necessary to avoid using the
          ‘sendfile’ system call even if the platform supports it.  This
          can be accomplished by by setting environment variable
          ‘AIOHTTP_NOSENDFILE=1’.

          If a Brotli or gzip compressed version of the static content
          exists at the requested path with the ‘.br’ or ‘.gz’
          extension, it will be used for the response.  Brotli will be
          preferred over gzip if both files exist.

               Warning: Use *note add_static(): 2a3. for development
               only.  In production, static content should be processed
               by web servers like 'nginx' or 'apache'.  Such web
               servers will be able to provide significantly better
               performance and security for static assets.  Several past
               security vulnerabilities in aiohttp only affected
               applications using *note add_static(): 2a3.


          Parameters:

             * ‘prefix’ (str(44)) – URL path prefix for handled static
               files

             * ‘path’ – path to the folder in file system that contains
               handled static files, str(45) or pathlib.Path(46).

             * ‘name’ (str(47)) – optional route name.

             * ‘expect_handler’ (collections.abc.Coroutine(48)) –
               optional 'expect' header handler.

             * ‘chunk_size’ (int(49)) – size of single chunk for file
               downloading, 256Kb by default.

               Increasing 'chunk_size' parameter to, say, 1Mb may
               increase file downloading speed but consumes more memory.

             * ‘show_index’ (bool(50)) – flag for allowing to show
               indexes of a directory, by default it’s not allowed and
               HTTP/403 will be returned on directory access.

             * ‘follow_symlinks’ (bool(51)) – flag for allowing to
               follow symlinks that lead outside the static root
               directory, by default it’s not allowed and HTTP/404 will
               be returned on access.  Enabling ‘follow_symlinks’ can be
               a security risk, and may lead to a directory transversal
               attack.  You do NOT need this option to follow symlinks
               which point to somewhere else within the static
               directory, this option is only used to break out of the
