13.5.1.28 async/await everywhere
................................

The main change is dropping ‘yield from’ support and using
‘async’/‘await’ everywhere.  Farewell, Python 3.4.

The minimal supported Python version is '3.5.3' now.

Why not '3.5.0'?  Because '3.5.3' has a crucial change:
asyncio.get_event_loop()(1) returns the running loop instead of
'default', which may be different, e.g.:

     loop = asyncio.new_event_loop()
     loop.run_until_complete(f())

Note, asyncio.set_event_loop()(2) was not called and default loop is not
equal to actually executed one.

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

   (1) 
https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-eventloop.html#asyncio.get_event_loop

   (2) 
https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-eventloop.html#asyncio.set_event_loop


File: aiohttp.info,  Node: Application Runners,  Next: Client Tracing<2>,  Prev: async/await everywhere,  Up: What’s new in aiohttp 3 0

13.5.1.29 Application Runners
.............................

People constantly asked about ability to run aiohttp servers together
with other asyncio code, but *note aiohttp.web.run_app(): 19e. is
blocking synchronous call.

aiohttp had support for starting the application without ‘run_app’ but
the API was very low-level and cumbersome.

Now application runners solve the task in a few lines of code, see *note
Application runners: 1a1. for details.


File: aiohttp.info,  Node: Client Tracing<2>,  Next: HTTPS support,  Prev: Application Runners,  Up: What’s new in aiohttp 3 0

13.5.1.30 Client Tracing
........................

Other long awaited feature is tracing client request life cycle to
figure out when and why client request spends a time waiting for
connection establishment, getting server response headers etc.

Now it is possible by registering special signal handlers on every
request processing stage.  *note Client Tracing: 6b. provides more info
about the feature.


File: aiohttp.info,  Node: HTTPS support,  Next: Dropped obsolete API,  Prev: Client Tracing<2>,  Up: What’s new in aiohttp 3 0

13.5.1.31 HTTPS support
.......................

Unfortunately asyncio has a bug with checking SSL certificates for
non-ASCII site DNS names, e.g.  ‘https://историк.рф’ or ‘https://雜草工
作室.香港’.

The bug has been fixed in upcoming Python 3.7 only (the change requires
breaking backward compatibility in ssl(1) API).

aiohttp installs a fix for older Python versions (3.5 and 3.6).

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

   (1) https://docs.python.org/3/library/ssl.html#module-ssl


